#!/bin/sh
# Governance gate, rung 3 (commit). See
# docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-26-governance-prevention-over-audit.md.
#
# This hook invokes ONE command and contains no check logic of its own. That is
# a sealed invariant, not a style choice: pre-commit, pre-push and CI all call
# the same entry point, which is what makes "passes locally, fails in CI"
# structurally impossible. If you are tempted to add a check here, add it to
# scripts/audit-lint.js instead.
#
# Budget: lint only, under two seconds (0.51s at writing). Heavier work belongs
# at pre-push or in CI. A slow pre-commit trains --no-verify, and a trained
# --no-verify is the end of the whole ladder.
#
# `--quiet` prints nothing unless something blocks. The advisory tally is
# expected to sit non-zero permanently, so echoing it on every commit would be
# a standing notice nobody reads. Advisories still appear on a bare
# `npm run lint:docs` — the session-close path where triaging them is the duty.
#
# WORKTREE LIMIT, measured 2026-07-27: `core.hooksPath` is relative, so git
# looks for this file inside the worktree's OWN checkout. A worktree cut from a
# commit that has `hooks/` is gated (verified: a fresh one blocked correctly);
# a worktree on a branch predating this directory has no hook at all and is
# silently ungated (verified against `war-model-slice2-ticket07`). CI is the
# backstop for exactly that case, which is why it is not optional.
exec npm run --silent lint:docs -- --quiet
